\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 denote the actual or true poverty indicator for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 geographical entity.
 Further, let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{p}$
\end_inset

 denote the direct estimate and model-based prediction of the poverty indicator,
 respectively.
 The standard method for assessing the predictive performance of model-based
 poverty estimates is to calculate the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 from a regression like the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{d}=\alpha+\beta y_{i}^{p}+\varepsilon_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 are the regression coefficients and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

 represents the error term.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that any regression of this form can only be estimated for the sampled
 entities with direct estimates.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 for the above regression can be written as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{d}^{2}=\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}\label{eq:r2direct}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of the direct estimate and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 thus conveys information about how much of the variance of the direct estimate
 is explained by the model-based prediction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While unbiased, direct estimates can be imprecise estimates of true poverty
 indicators 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "rodas2021pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Ideally, one would assess the performance of model-based predictions on
 the basis of the true poverty indicators themselves.
 That is, model-based predictions are more appropriately assessed using
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 from the following regression:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{a}=\alpha+\beta y_{i}^{p}+\eta_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

 represents the error term.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 for this regression can written as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{a}^{2}=\frac{\sigma_{a}^{2}-\sigma_{\eta}^{2}}{\sigma_{a}^{2}}\label{eq:r2true}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of the true poverty indicator and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\eta}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

.
 In what follows, we characterize the bias of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 when it is used to assess true quantity of interest 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}=y_{i}^{a}+\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 is a mean-zero error term with variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 That is, we view this as a classical measurement error problem where the
 direct estimate of the poverty indicator is a noisy estimate of the true
 poverty indicator.
 Note that the preceding equation implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{i}=\eta_{i}+\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}=\sigma_{\eta}^{2}+\sigma_{\omega}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "majeske2010quantifying"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, we then define the bias as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B=R_{a}^{2}-R_{d}^{2}.\label{eq:bias}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting Eqs.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2direct"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2true"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) into our expression for the bias, we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B=\frac{\sigma_{a}^{2}-\sigma_{\eta}^{2}}{\sigma_{a}^{2}}-\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\eta}^{2}-\sigma_{\omega}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}=\frac{\sigma_{\omega}^{2}(\sigma_{a}^{2}-\sigma_{\eta}^{2})}{\sigma_{d}^{2}\sigma_{a}^{2}}=\frac{\sigma_{\omega}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}R_{a}^{2}.\label{eq:bias_res}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Finally, we can substitute Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:bias_res"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) into Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:bias"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) and rearrange, which gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{d}^{2}=R_{a}^{2}\left(\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\omega}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}\right)\label{eq:relationship}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and establishes a basic relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "majeske2010quantifying"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for additional mathematical details.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:relationship"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) above, we see that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}=R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega}^{2}=0$
\end_inset

 or, equivalently, when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}=y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 In the presence of measurement error or when 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega}^{2}>0$
\end_inset

, we then see that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 will be biased downward and the magnitude of the bias will be determined
 by the size of 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\omega}^{2})/\sigma_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Validation exercises based on 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 will thus tend to mischaracterize the performance of model-based approaches
 to poverty mapping, with the resulting estimates being systematically biased
 downward (see Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:standard_implementation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 for estimates of the bias).
 This characterization of the bias associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is especially critical for the common practice of comparing models across
 contexts because the variances of the direct estimates and measurement
 errors will generally differ across datasets.
 In particular, a poorly performing model applied to a low bias setting
 may yield an estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 that is higher than an estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 from a strong model applied to a high bias setting.
 The presence of bias can thus reverse the rank ordering of models when
 comparisons are made across contexts.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is an additional form of bias in 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 that can lead to overoptimistic conclusions related to model validity.
 To illustrate this, note that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 in Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2true"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) is mathematically equivalent to the squared (Pearson) correlation coefficient
 between 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{p}$
\end_inset

.
 A well-known property of the correlation coefficient is that it is invariant
 to any positive affine transformation of its arguments, implying that the
 squared correlation coefficient is invariant to any affine transformation
 of its arguments 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "gujarati2003basic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
More specifically, the correlation coefficient is invariant to any 
\shape italic
positive
\shape default
 affine transformation because it changes signs when one of its arguments
 is scaled by some negative constant.
 The 
\shape italic
squared
\shape default
 correlation coefficient is then invariant to any affine transformation.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We can thus write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{a}^{2}(y_{i}^{a},y_{i}^{p})=R_{a}^{2}(y_{i}^{a},c_{1}+c_{2}y_{i}^{p})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{2}$
\end_inset

 are some arbitrary constants.
 The primary implication of the above is that it implies that the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 is insensitive to bias in the model-based poverty estimates.
 For example, consider some unbiased poverty estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

, meaning that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}^{u}-y_{i}^{a})=0.$
\end_inset

 Now consider another estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}$
\end_inset

 that is identical to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

 up to some locational shift, implying that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}=y_{i}^{u}+c$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}^{b}-y_{i}^{a})=c$
\end_inset

.
 While 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}$
\end_inset

 is biased (i.e., it will systematically over- or under-estimate poverty),
 it will achieve an 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 that is identical to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A final issue related to 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 as a performance metric is that it is affected by the variance of the true
 poverty measures.
 To see this, consider the following re-expression of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{a}^{2}=1-\frac{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}(y_{i}^{p}-y_{i}^{a})^{2}}{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}(y_{i}^{a}-\bar{{y}}^{a})^{2}}=1-\frac{\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{p},y_{i}^{a})}{\text{{Var}}(y_{i}^{a})}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the number of geographical entities.
 The above shows that 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be viewed as a normalized version of the mean squared error with the
 variance of the true poverty indicator used as the normalization factor.
 This approach to normalization, however, can be misleading when comparing
 models across contexts (i.e., across countries or regions within a country).
 Specifically, consider two models that perform equally well in that they
 achieve the same mean squared error.
 However, assume that one model is applied to a context where the variance
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 is large and the other is applied to a low variance context.
 The above then implies that the model applied to the high variance context
 will achieve a higher 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

, even though the performance of the two models is arguably identical.
 
\end_layout

